1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,895
Here's a genuinely unusual thing the app does, worth understanding: how it lets you connect ten MCP servers without drowning the model in tool definitions.

2
00:00:04,895 --> 00:00:06,000
It's called progressive disclosure.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:06,981
The naive problem first.

4
00:00:06,981 --> 00:00:09,558
Every tool the model can use has to be described in the prompt.

5
00:00:09,558 --> 00:00:17,000
Connect several servers with dozens of tools each, and that's a huge, ever-growing block of definitions sent on every single request — expensive, and it makes the model choose worse.

6
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:20,787
The app's fix: instead of exposing every server's tools, it exposes just four small meta-tools.

7
00:00:20,787 --> 00:00:22,183
mcp_servers lists what's connected.

8
00:00:22,183 --> 00:00:23,777
mcp_tools lists the tools on one server.

9
00:00:23,777 --> 00:00:25,731
mcp_describe gets the details of a specific tool.

10
00:00:25,731 --> 00:00:26,927
And mcp_call actually runs it.

11
00:00:26,927 --> 00:00:29,000
The model discovers what it needs, when it needs it.

12
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:29,636
Watch it live.

13
00:00:29,636 --> 00:00:31,636
Ask for something a connected server can do.

14
00:00:31,636 --> 00:00:38,227
The model calls mcp_servers to see what's available, then mcp_tools to find the right one, then mcp_call to run it — narrowing down step by step.

15
00:00:38,227 --> 00:00:41,000
Only the tools it actually needs ever enter the conversation.

16
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,635
The tradeoff, said out loud: this costs an extra round-trip or two before the first real action, while the model looks around.

17
00:00:45,635 --> 00:00:48,615
For one server it's not worth it; past about two connected servers it clearly is.

18
00:00:48,615 --> 00:00:52,000
The app makes the right call automatically, but it's good to know why there's a small pause.

19
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:53,445
Now you understand how it scales.

20
00:00:53,445 --> 00:00:58,000
Last in the MCP module: fast triage for the three things that actually go wrong when connecting servers.

